Grade 1 intrinsic muscle strain from lifting heavy box to lumbar spine
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Localized paraspinal ache after lifting, Pain worsened by forward flexion, Complete absence of radicular symptoms

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-007
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of severe lower back pain post-lifting, requiring urgent assessment to exclude cauda equina syndrome or spinal fracture.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted acute, severely limiting lower back pain after a heavy lifting maneuver. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to rule out spinal cord compression, neurovascular deficit, or traumatic disc herniation prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I lifted a heavy box and my back seized up immediately, it constantly aches now.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms presented with an acute onset of localized lower back ache and severe spasm immediately following lifting a heavy object. The pain is characterized as a dull ache confined solely to the lumbar paraspinal musculature. Symptoms are severely exacerbated by active spinal flexion and marginally relieved by rest in a supine position. There is a complete absence of pain, tingling, or numbness radiating down the buttocks or legs. The patient denies any recent bowel or bladder incontinence or retention.
Functional Status: Mobilizing with extreme caution; unable to flex the spine.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; temporary severe restriction in activities of daily living and self-care.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/88 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 90 Kg
General: Alert, maintaining a rigidly upright posture, demonstrating significant discomfort during positional transitions.
Physical Exam: Lumbar spine examination reveals localized paraspinal ache. There is mild to moderate muscular tightness and palpable hypertonicity in the erector spinae group bilaterally. Forward flexion is restricted to 20 degrees by pain. Complete absence of focal midline spinous process tenderness. Lower limb neurological examination is entirely normal: full motor strength and bulk testing, intact deep tendon reflexes, and preserved sensory dermatomes. Straight leg raise and crossed straight leg raise tests are negative for dural tension. Perineal sensation is intact.
Environmental Observations: Residence is structurally sound but cluttered, which necessitates cautious navigation.
Medication Reconciliation: Took one dose of paracetamol prior to arrival; nil other medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Grade 1 intrinsic muscle strain of lumbar spine - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Lumbar disc herniation - Ruled out due to: Complete absence of radicular symptoms, normal motor/sensory exam, and negative straight leg raise.

Cauda equina syndrome - Ruled out due to: Intact bowel/bladder function, normal perineal sensation, and bilateral normal lower limb neurology.

Vertebral compression fracture - Ruled out due to: Lack of severe midline bony point tenderness and absence of high-risk mechanism (e.g., severe trauma, known osteoporosis).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient’s acute onset of severe low back pain after lifting mandated an immediate on-site neurological and musculoskeletal evaluation. Bedside assessment confidently excluded cauda equina syndrome, significant disc herniation, and traumatic bony injury, confirming isolated paraspinal hypertonicity. Urgent after-hours assessment was definitively required to rule out these major neurological emergencies, provide immediate and effective muscle relaxant/analgesic intervention, and prevent unnecessary ambulance transport to the emergency department, safely bridging the patient to standard daytime primary care.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral NSAIDs and a short course of muscle relaxants to disrupt the acute spasm cycle. Standard lumbar spine radiographs are clinically unindicated.
Positive Behavioral Change: Alternate between short periods of walking and resting supine with knees elevated. This dynamic positional shifting prevents severe muscle stiffening and accelerates functional recovery.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Heat packs.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 to 5 days to assess recovery and discuss manual handling techniques.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Call 000 or proceed immediately to the Emergency Department if you lose control of your bladder or bowels, experience numbness around your groin, or develop progressive weakness in your legs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; underlying degenerative changes are common but incidental to acute muscular spasm.
Clinical Course: Severe spasm typically peaks within 24-48 hours and then resolves gradually over 1 to 2 weeks with appropriate mobility.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if you suddenly cannot pass urine, lose bowel control, or if your legs become dangerously weak.

